Question 1: Partitioning

Step 1: Create the partitioned table and insert records

  SQL

  CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS prac;
  USE prac;

  CREATE TABLE product (
    product_id INT,
    product_name VARCHAR(30),
    category VARCHAR(20),
    price INT
  )
  PARTITION BY HASH (price)
  PARTITIONS 4;

  INSERT INTO product VALUES
  (1,'Laptop','Electronics',1500),
  (2,'Mouse','Electronics',751),
  (3,'Pen','Stationery',1002),
  (4,'Notebook','Stationery',1201),
  (5,'Keyboard','Electronics',500),
  (6,'Bag','Stationery',450);

Step 2: Display records of each partition

  SQL

  SELECT * FROM product PARTITION (p0);
  SELECT * FROM product PARTITION (p1);
  SELECT * FROM product PARTITION (p2);
  SELECT * FROM product PARTITION (p3);

  SELECT PARTITION_NAME, PARTITION_METHOD, PARTITION_DESCRIPTION, TABLE_ROWS
  FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.PARTITIONS
  WHERE TABLE_SCHEMA = 'prac' AND TABLE_NAME = 'product';

Step 3: DML operations (UPDATE and DELETE)

  SQL

  UPDATE product SET price = 1203 WHERE product_id = 2;
  UPDATE product SET category = 'Office Supplies' WHERE product_id = 4;
  DELETE FROM product WHERE product_id = 5;

Step 4: Display each partition again

  SQL

  SELECT * FROM product PARTITION (p0);
  SELECT * FROM product PARTITION (p1);
  SELECT * FROM product PARTITION (p2);
  SELECT * FROM product PARTITION (p3);

Partition-wise data storage
 • Partitioning: one logical table is split into smaller physical parts called partitions, based on
   a partition key column (here price).
 • Range partitioning: each partition holds rows within a range of the key, for example price
   below 500 in p0, 500 to 999 in p1, and so on.
 • Hash partitioning: MySQL computes (key value MOD number of partitions) and the result
   decides the partition. Here price MOD 4 is used, so 1500 goes to p0 and 751 to p3, and rows
   spread evenly.
 • Working: on INSERT, MySQL stores the row in the matching partition automatically; on
   UPDATE of the key, the row moves to the new partition. A query that filters on the key reads
   only the needed partition (partition pruning), which is the advantage of partition-wise
   retrieval: it is faster and rows are spread evenly.

Question 2: Distributed Transactions

Distributed transaction
A distributed transaction is a transaction that updates data in more than one database (or site)
and must still behave as a single unit: either all the updates are saved or none of them
(atomicity). The order is placed in the Shop database and the payment is recorded in the Pay
database. If payment fails, the order must not stay.

Example: Online shopping across two databases (Shop and Pay)

  SQL

  CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS Shop;
  CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS Pay;

  CREATE TABLE Shop.orders (order_id INT, product VARCHAR(20), status VARCHAR(10));
  CREATE TABLE Pay.payment (pay_id INT, order_id INT, amount INT, status VARCHAR(10));

  START TRANSACTION;
  INSERT INTO Shop.orders VALUES (1,'Mouse','PLACED');
  INSERT INTO Pay.payment VALUES (101,1,499,'PAID');
  SELECT * FROM Shop.orders;
  SELECT * FROM Pay.payment;
  COMMIT;

  SELECT * FROM Shop.orders;
  SELECT * FROM Pay.payment;

Purpose of COMMIT and ROLLBACK
 • COMMIT: saves all the changes of the transaction permanently in all the databases. Used
   when every step of the transaction succeeds. The database moves to a new consistent state
   and both databases show the completed work.
 • ROLLBACK: cancels all the changes of the transaction in all the databases. Used when any
   step fails or the user cancels. The database returns to the state before START
   TRANSACTION, so no half-done work remains and data stays consistent.

Why transaction recovery is required
1. To ensure Atomicity. A transaction must be completed fully or not at all. If a failure occurs
   after some operations, the completed operations must be undone (ROLLBACK).
2. To handle different types of failures. Transactions can fail due to a system crash, power
   failure, disk failure, network failure between the databases, deadlock or logical errors. The
   DBMS must recover from all of them.
3. To maintain Consistency of the database. Partial updates leave the databases in an
   incorrect state, for example money debited in one database but not credited in the other.
   Recovery brings them back to a consistent state.
4. To ensure Durability. Once a transaction is committed, its changes must remain
   permanent even if the system crashes immediately afterwards. Recovery uses the log file to
   redo committed transactions.

5. To avoid wrong data being used by other transactions. If a failed transaction's
   uncommitted changes remain, other transactions may read incorrect values. Recovery
   removes these changes.

Conclusion: Transaction recovery protects the ACID properties and keeps the databases
correct and reliable after any failure.

Question 3: Star Schema vs Snowflake Schema

Introduction
A data warehouse stores data in a fact table (numeric measures such as amount) surrounded
by dimension tables (who, what, when). The way dimensions are arranged gives two
schemas. A star schema keeps each dimension as one denormalised table. A snowflake
schema normalises the dimensions into more tables (normalisation removes redundancy by
splitting a table into related tables).

Star Schema (diagram)

               Dim_Customer                                                                Dim_Product
          customer_id (PK)                                                            product_id (PK)
          name                                                                        product_name
          city                                                                        category
          state                                                                       brand
                                                        Fact_Sales
                                               sales_id (PK)
                                               customer_id (FK)
                                               product_id (FK)
                                               date_id (FK)
                                               amount

                                                        Dim_Date
                                               date_id (PK)
                                               order_date
                                               month
                                                year
Fact table in the centre, dimension tables joined directly. Dimensions are flat (denormalised).

Who bought: Dim_Customer. What was sold: Dim_Product. When: Dim_Date. City, state,
category, month and year sit inside the dimension tables.

Snowflake Schema (diagram)

                   Dim_City                                                           Dim_Category
          city_id (PK)                                                           category_id (PK)
          city_name                                                              category_name
          state_name
                                                       Fact_Sales
                                              sales_id (PK)
               Dim_Customer                                                           Dim_Product
                                              customer_id (FK)
          customer_id (PK)                                                       product_id (PK)
                                              product_id (FK)
          name                                                                   product_name
                                              date_id (FK)
          city_id (FK)                                                           category_id (FK)
                                              amount

                                                       Dim_Date
                                              date_id (PK)
                                              order_date
                                              month
                                            year
Dimensions are normalised further: Customer > City, Product > Category.

Differences (write any four)

  Point                   Star Schema                                     Snowflake Schema

  Structure               Fact table in the centre, dimensions            Dimensions split into sub-dimensions
                          joined directly (star shape).                   (snowflake shape).

  Dimension               Single, denormalised table per dimension.       Normalised into several related
  tables                                                                  tables.

  Redundancy              More redundancy (city, state repeat in          Less redundancy, data stored once.
                          every row).

  Storage                 Needs more storage.                             Needs less storage.

  Query                   Faster, fewer joins (2 in the example).         Slower, more joins (4 in the
  performance                                                             example).

Example with SQL (same data, same result)
Star schema: 3 dimension tables, query uses 2 joins.

  SQL

  DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS star_db;
  CREATE DATABASE star_db;
  USE star_db;

  CREATE TABLE Dim_Customer (customer_id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(50), city VARCHAR(30),
       state VARCHAR(30));
  CREATE TABLE Dim_Product (product_id INT PRIMARY KEY, product_name VARCHAR(50),
       category VARCHAR(30), brand VARCHAR(30));
  CREATE TABLE Dim_Date (date_id INT PRIMARY KEY, order_date DATE, month INT, year INT);
  CREATE TABLE Fact_Sales (
     sales_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
     customer_id INT, product_id INT, date_id INT, amount DECIMAL(10,2),
     FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES Dim_Customer(customer_id),
     FOREIGN KEY (product_id) REFERENCES Dim_Product(product_id),
     FOREIGN KEY (date_id) REFERENCES Dim_Date(date_id)
  );

  INSERT INTO Dim_Customer VALUES (1,'Rahul','Pune','Maharashtra'),(2,'Amit','Bhopal','MP');
  INSERT INTO Dim_Product VALUES (1,'Laptop','Electronics','Asus'),(2,'Pen','Stationery','Cello');
  INSERT INTO Dim_Date VALUES (1,'2026-11-01',11,2026),(2,'2026-12-01',12,2026);
  INSERT INTO Fact_Sales VALUES (1,1,1,1,50000),(2,2,2,2,100),(3,1,2,2,50);

  SELECT * FROM Dim_Customer;
  SELECT * FROM Dim_Product;
  SELECT * FROM Dim_Date;
  SELECT * FROM Fact_Sales;

  SELECT
      F.Sales_ID,
      C.Name,
      P.Category,
      C.State,
      F.Amount
  FROM Fact_Sales F
  JOIN Dim_Customer C ON F.Customer_ID = C.Customer_ID
  JOIN Dim_Product P ON F.Product_ID = P.Product_ID;

Snowflake schema
Snowflake schema: dimensions normalised, the same query needs 4 joins.

  SQL

  DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS snow_db;
  CREATE DATABASE snow_db;
  USE snow_db;

  CREATE TABLE Dim_City (city_id INT PRIMARY KEY, city_name VARCHAR(30), state_name VARCHAR(30));
  CREATE TABLE Dim_Customer (customer_id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(50), city_id INT,
     FOREIGN KEY (city_id) REFERENCES Dim_City(city_id));
  CREATE TABLE Dim_Category (category_id INT PRIMARY KEY, category_name VARCHAR(30));
  CREATE TABLE Dim_Product (product_id INT PRIMARY KEY, product_name VARCHAR(50), category_id INT,
     FOREIGN KEY (category_id) REFERENCES Dim_Category(category_id));
  CREATE TABLE Dim_Date (date_id INT PRIMARY KEY, order_date DATE, month INT, year INT);
  CREATE TABLE Fact_Sales (
     sales_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
     customer_id INT, product_id INT, date_id INT, amount DECIMAL(10,2),
     FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES Dim_Customer(customer_id),
     FOREIGN KEY (product_id) REFERENCES Dim_Product(product_id),
     FOREIGN KEY (date_id) REFERENCES Dim_Date(date_id)
  );

  INSERT INTO Dim_City VALUES (1,'Pune','Maharashtra'),(2,'Bhopal','MP');
  INSERT INTO Dim_Customer VALUES (1,'Rahul',1),(2,'Amit',2);
  INSERT INTO Dim_Category VALUES (1,'Electronics'),(2,'Stationery');
  INSERT INTO Dim_Product VALUES (1,'Laptop',1),(2,'Pen',2);
  INSERT INTO Dim_Date VALUES (1,'2026-11-01',11,2026),(2,'2026-12-01',12,2026);
  INSERT INTO Fact_Sales VALUES (1,1,1,1,50000),(2,2,2,2,100),(3,1,2,2,50);

  SELECT * FROM Dim_City;
  SELECT * FROM Dim_Customer;
  SELECT * FROM Dim_Category;
  SELECT * FROM Dim_Product;
  SELECT * FROM Dim_Date;
  SELECT * FROM Fact_Sales;

  SELECT
      F.Sales_ID,
      C.Name,
      Cat.Category_Name,
      Ci.State_Name,
      F.Amount
  FROM Fact_Sales F
  JOIN Dim_Customer C ON F.Customer_ID = C.Customer_ID
  JOIN Dim_City Ci ON C.City_ID = Ci.City_ID
  JOIN Dim_Product P ON F.Product_ID = P.Product_ID
  JOIN Dim_Category Cat ON P.Category_ID = Cat.Category_ID;

Conclusion
Star schema is simpler and faster for queries, snowflake saves storage and reduces
redundancy but needs more joins. The same question was answered with 2 joins in star and 4
joins in snowflake.

